{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe mean reversion: When history shows a sharp spike (e.g., >75) followed by a drop, the model's smooth upward drift significantly overestimates prices; apply a downward correction of 5-10% in the first 10 days of the forecast horizon.",
    "Adjust for sentiment lag: If sentiment history is mixed or declining (values < 0.67) while the model forecasts a steady rise, the model fails to price in immediate downside risk; reduce the forecast slope by 50% or flatten it to the recent 18-day average.",
    "Cap overshoot after volatility: In periods of high volatility (e.g., swings between 65-76), the model consistently overshoots the peak and undershoots the trough; flatten the forecast curve to match the amplitude of the last 18-day range rather than projecting a monotonic trend.",
    "Horizon-specific bias: The model exhibits a persistent positive bias in the short term (days 1-5) when the immediate history is rising; subtract 1-2 EUR from the first 5 forecasted days to align with the actual pullback observed in the true outcomes.",
    "Sentiment-weighted reversion: When sentiment history contains negative values (e.g., -0.50, -1.00) immediately preceding the forecast, the model's assumption of stability is invalid; force a mean reversion to the 18-day moving average within the first 3 days of the forecast."
  ]
}
